<p>Google is changing parts of Search in Europe to comply with the EU Digital Markets Act. The changes can affect how vertical search services and direct business links appear, so international SEO teams need to separate European SERP behavior from global ranking changes.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/google-europe-search-dma-september-seo-2026.svg" alt="Diagram showing European Google Search results changing under the Digital Markets Act"></figure>
<h2>What Google changed in Europe</h2><p>Reuters reported on September 8 that Google was changing European Search after a €460 million EU fine. The new format gives more visibility to vertical search services such as travel platforms in some result types and changes how information is displayed.</p><h2>Why this is an SEO reporting problem</h2><p>A global rank report can make a European traffic change look like a ranking loss. But if the SERP layout changed, the click distribution can change even when a page keeps a similar organic position.</p>
<h2>What SEOs should segment</h2><ul><li>Country and market.</li><li>Query type and search intent.</li><li>Organic clicks and impressions.</li><li>SERP feature presence.</li><li>Direct and vertical-search competitors.</li></ul>
<h2>How to investigate a traffic drop</h2><ol><li>Compare Europe with unaffected markets.</li><li>Check Search Console by country and query.</li><li>Inspect the current SERP for important queries.</li><li>Separate feature changes from ranking changes.</li><li>Compare conversions, not only clicks.</li></ol>
<h2>Travel and local businesses need extra care</h2><p>Businesses that depend on direct booking or local discovery can be affected when a vertical search service gets more prominent placement. Track branded and non-branded queries separately and monitor the path from search impression to booking or lead.</p>
<h2>Do not overreact with technical changes</h2><p>A different SERP layout does not automatically require new schema or a new page. First confirm that Google can crawl and understand the existing page, then determine whether the change is about eligibility, presentation or ranking.</p>
<h2>Build a market-specific SEO dashboard</h2><p>Keep country-level Search Console data, SERP notes and conversion data together. Add an annotation when Google changes a regional Search experience. This makes future reporting much easier.</p>
<h2>Related ToolBoxKart guides</h2><p>For the earlier regional Search documentation update, read <a href="/blog/google-regional-search-experience-what-seos-need-to-check">Google Regional Search Experience</a>. For ranking changes, use <a href="/blog/google-core-update-tracker">Google Core Update Tracker</a>. For AI visibility, see <a href="/blog/google-ai-search-visibility-report">Google AI Search Visibility Report</a>. For country-level Search Console analysis, use <a href="/blog/search-console-ai-performance-report-guide">the Search Console AI Performance Report guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does the EU Search change affect every country?</h3><p>The reported changes are focused on European users and should not be treated as a global SERP change.</p><h3>Can a SERP layout change reduce clicks without a ranking loss?</h3><p>Yes. Changing feature placement can change click distribution even when organic positions are stable.</p><h3>Should every European site change its SEO strategy?</h3><p>No. Start with country-level evidence and query-level SERP checks before making changes.</p>
